WEBVTT


00:01:40.681 --> 00:01:42.201
It's really steep.


00:01:56.481 --> 00:01:59.681
Huge fan, huge fan. -Would you
like to have your photo taken?


00:01:59.801 --> 00:02:00.921
Can I have your picture?


00:02:01.041 --> 00:02:02.681
Come on, you guys.
Want to have your picture?


00:02:02.801 --> 00:02:05.081
- Here you go.
- Pleased to have you here.


00:02:06.681 --> 00:02:08.041
Cheesy grin.


00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:10.441
Smile.


00:02:10.561 --> 00:02:12.081
Another cheesy grin.


00:02:13.161 --> 00:02:17.241
All the great scientists
and this is one of 'em - Kepler and...


00:02:17.361 --> 00:02:20.681
Galileo, Newton - we've got one
right here. It's fantastic. Amazing!


00:02:23.161 --> 00:02:24.921
<i>My name is Stephen Hawking.</i>


00:02:28.521 --> 00:02:32.841
<i>For the past 50 years,
I have travelled the world</i>


00:02:32.961 --> 00:02:35.801
<i>studying and lecturing
about time and space</i>


00:02:35.921 --> 00:02:38.761
<i>and the laws that govern
the universe.</i>


00:02:43.921 --> 00:02:47.921
- Hey, Stephen, break a leg.
- Don't say that!


00:02:48.041 --> 00:02:49.801
I'll have to look after him.


00:03:03.241 --> 00:03:07.841
<i>This film is a personal journey
through my life</i>


00:03:07.961 --> 00:03:09.361
<i>told in my own words.</i>


00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:15.401
<i>Come with me and I will show you
how I live and work today...</i>


00:03:16.481 --> 00:03:20.241
<i>...and tell you the story
of how I became who I am.</i>


00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:30.961
<i>Welcome to my world.</i>


00:04:38.801 --> 00:04:41.841
So are you going to take your lunch
in the cafeteria?


00:04:45.721 --> 00:04:47.121
- It's up to you.
- Yes.


00:04:47.241 --> 00:04:48.441
I just have to give Judith a call...


00:04:48.561 --> 00:04:50.201
<i>I think it would kill him</i>


00:04:50.321 --> 00:04:54.001
<i>if he was in a home,
being cared for by nurses.</i>


00:04:54.121 --> 00:04:57.241
<i>I think that would be the end for him.</i>


00:04:57.361 --> 00:05:00.361
<i>He likes to be able to choose
what he wants to do with his life,</i>


00:05:00.481 --> 00:05:04.081
<i>he loves the danger of flying,
he wants to go into space</i>


00:05:04.201 --> 00:05:06.081
<i>and he's been in submarines!</i>


00:05:06.201 --> 00:05:08.041
He's just the most craziest man.


00:05:08.161 --> 00:05:10.361
He's got a lot of guts in him.


00:05:17.041 --> 00:05:18.681
Just through here is
the nurses' room.


00:05:18.801 --> 00:05:23.561
So if anything medical happens,
we'll just grab a phone, phone 999,


00:05:23.681 --> 00:05:27.081
but there is not much
we can actually do


00:05:27.201 --> 00:05:30.641
because Stephen is on a Nippy,
which he'll breathe on.


00:05:30.761 --> 00:05:34.921
If that fails, the only other thing
we could do and we do have -


00:05:35.041 --> 00:05:39.041
we have oxygen cylinders
and we can give him oxygen,


00:05:39.161 --> 00:05:43.321
but if he's in a total state
then we will have to let him go.


00:05:53.321 --> 00:05:57.001
<i>I have lived
over two thirds of my life</i>


00:05:57.121 --> 00:05:59.321
<i>with the threat of death
hanging over me.</i>


00:06:05.001 --> 00:06:08.841
<i>Because every new day
could be my last,</i>


00:06:08.961 --> 00:06:14.041
<i>I have developed a desire to make
the most of each and every minute.</i>


00:06:21.121 --> 00:06:23.681
<i>Although I'm 71 now,</i>


00:06:23.801 --> 00:06:27.921
<i>I still go to work every day
at Cambridge University.</i>


00:06:28.041 --> 00:06:31.161
I'll see you in a bit.
Enjoy your shopping.


00:06:31.281 --> 00:06:35.361
<i>Keeping an active mind
has been vital to my survival,</i>


00:06:35.481 --> 00:06:39.841
<i>as has been maintaining
a sense of humour.</i>


00:06:39.961 --> 00:06:41.321
- Morning, Judith.
- Hello.


00:06:44.681 --> 00:06:47.801
<i>When I went to my job interview,</i>


00:06:47.921 --> 00:06:49.441
<i>I thought he was going to ask me</i>


00:06:49.561 --> 00:06:53.321
<i>about my past medical history
and what I've done before in care,</i>


00:06:53.441 --> 00:06:56.441
<i>but he didn't, he asked
whether I could cook poached eggs!</i>


00:06:56.561 --> 00:07:02.321
And... I was 19 at the time
and I lied,


00:07:02.441 --> 00:07:07.001
cos I didn't know how to cook poached
eggs, but I got the job straightaway.


00:07:09.761 --> 00:07:12.721
Er... it ain't like going to work any more,


00:07:12.841 --> 00:07:15.161
it's like just gonna go and see
a friend...


00:07:15.281 --> 00:07:16.721
well, family.


00:07:16.841 --> 00:07:19.081
Stephen's been there
through the years of me growing up


00:07:19.201 --> 00:07:20.601
and, like, turning into an adult.


00:07:20.721 --> 00:07:26.361
So he's been there through most
of the important stages of my life.


00:07:28.241 --> 00:07:31.761
I've had a very privileged life,
thanks to Stephen.


00:07:32.961 --> 00:07:35.081
Do you enjoy my company?


00:07:36.361 --> 00:07:39.161
Maybe.


00:07:41.401 --> 00:07:43.481
You're horrible to me, ain't ya?


00:07:43.601 --> 00:07:46.121
Yes.


00:07:46.241 --> 00:07:50.201
<i>As you can see,
the gradual advance of my illness</i>


00:07:50.321 --> 00:07:55.081
<i>has meant that I am totally reliant
on those around me.</i>


00:07:58.441 --> 00:08:02.761
<i>71 years ago,
life started that way too.</i>


00:08:10.601 --> 00:08:17.601
<i>I was born in 1942, exactly 300 years
after the death of Galileo.</i>


00:08:19.001 --> 00:08:22.721
<i>My parents were
both at Oxford University,</i>


00:08:22.841 --> 00:08:25.561
<i>where my father studied medicine.</i>


00:08:25.681 --> 00:08:29.361
<i>He later specialised
in tropical diseases.</i>


00:08:30.761 --> 00:08:34.721
<i>It is often said
that a person's early years</i>


00:08:34.841 --> 00:08:38.081
<i>are a good indication
of how they will turn out.</i>


00:08:39.481 --> 00:08:43.441
<i>Perhaps my eldest sister, Mary,
remembers me best.</i>


00:08:44.561 --> 00:08:48.601
I remember Stephen was very
bright, always into things.


00:08:50.561 --> 00:08:52.721
I remember my father made me
a dolls' house,


00:08:52.841 --> 00:08:55.761
so Stephen put in both plumbing
and lighting!


00:08:59.481 --> 00:09:00.481
He liked to win.


00:09:02.281 --> 00:09:04.641
He liked to win in everything.


00:09:04.761 --> 00:09:07.201
We all learnt to play draughts.


00:09:07.321 --> 00:09:11.201
I beat Stephen once, once at draughts,


00:09:11.321 --> 00:09:14.441
and he immediately took up chess
and I never beat him at that again!


00:09:20.721 --> 00:09:25.801
<i>I also spent a lot of time
playing on my own as a boy.</i>


00:09:30.041 --> 00:09:33.361
<i>I had a passion to understand
how things worked,</i>


00:09:33.481 --> 00:09:36.321
<i>from toy trains
to the whole universe.</i>


00:09:41.441 --> 00:09:44.281
He would spend a lot of time
looking at the sky,


00:09:44.401 --> 00:09:45.441
looking at the, um...


00:09:45.561 --> 00:09:50.681
stars and wondering where...
eternity came to an end.


00:09:51.841 --> 00:09:55.521
He couldn't conceive
that there could be something


00:09:55.641 --> 00:09:57.721
without a finish.


00:10:02.121 --> 00:10:05.881
<i>Home life was always
stimulating for me and my siblings.</i>


00:10:07.201 --> 00:10:10.761
<i>My mother and father were intellectuals</i>


00:10:10.881 --> 00:10:14.921
<i>and naturally they expected
their children to follow.</i>


00:10:16.041 --> 00:10:19.681
Both my parents were moving
in the Highgate-Hampstead intelligentsia,


00:10:19.801 --> 00:10:22.041
that sort of circle.


00:10:23.881 --> 00:10:24.961
We always talked -


00:10:25.081 --> 00:10:27.841
everyone talked,
and everybody argued.


00:10:27.961 --> 00:10:30.561
We used to argue theology a lot.
It's a great thing for kids


00:10:30.681 --> 00:10:33.481
because you don't need
any facts whatsoever.


00:10:40.521 --> 00:10:44.921
<i>To outsiders, the Hawking
household was considered eccentric,</i>


00:10:45.041 --> 00:10:50.521
<i>but, for me, it was a place where
my mind was constantly challenged.</i>


00:10:52.001 --> 00:10:54.001
There were books everywhere.


00:10:54.121 --> 00:10:58.041
Bookshelves that were double-banked.
Bookshelves that had,


00:10:58.161 --> 00:10:59.521
on top of upright books,


00:10:59.641 --> 00:11:02.321
rows of other books shoehorned in
wherever there was space.


00:11:03.841 --> 00:11:05.561
It was a less conventional house -


00:11:05.681 --> 00:11:08.761
one in which the children had
a great deal of freedom


00:11:08.881 --> 00:11:09.961
and I remember being


00:11:10.081 --> 00:11:12.881
quite gob-smacked
by the conversation over lunch.


00:11:13.001 --> 00:11:17.001
It was about subjects which were
never talked about in my house -


00:11:17.121 --> 00:11:21.841
sex, homosexuality,
arguments for and against abortion


00:11:21.961 --> 00:11:25.761
and various other subjects
that were quite unusual.


00:11:27.201 --> 00:11:31.361
<i>As I developed into a teenager,
my parents taught me</i>


00:11:31.481 --> 00:11:34.441
<i>to always question things
and think big.</i>


00:11:35.841 --> 00:11:39.561
Stephen devised - and remember,
this was in the early 1950s -


00:11:39.681 --> 00:11:41.441
not just one, but two, computers,


00:11:41.561 --> 00:11:43.561
which we built from scratch.


00:11:43.681 --> 00:11:47.561
It was about the size
of a half-depth fridge,


00:11:47.681 --> 00:11:50.481
but about as tall and about as wide.


00:11:50.601 --> 00:11:54.601
It solved logical problems posed
in binary - if this condition is true


00:11:54.721 --> 00:11:59.201
and that condition is true or that
condition instead is true, then...


00:11:59.321 --> 00:12:02.161
and it would give you
what the "then" meant.


00:12:05.681 --> 00:12:09.441
<i>At school, my classmates
gave me the nickname Einstein,</i>


00:12:09.561 --> 00:12:12.841
<i>even though I was only ever
halfway up the class.</i>


00:12:14.401 --> 00:12:18.241
<i>It was, I like to think,
a very bright class.</i>


00:12:28.481 --> 00:12:32.601
It was always assumed
that Stephen would go to Oxford.


00:12:32.721 --> 00:12:36.481
Both my parents had been to Oxford,
you know, he was extremely bright,


00:12:36.601 --> 00:12:39.161
there was never
any doubt about this.


00:12:39.281 --> 00:12:44.201
He and my father had a difference
of opinion about what he should study.


00:12:44.321 --> 00:12:47.521
Father thought it would be a good thing
if Stephen did medicine,


00:12:47.641 --> 00:12:49.841
but Stephen was not into medicine -


00:12:49.961 --> 00:12:52.601
he'd have made
the most awful doctor...


00:12:53.721 --> 00:12:54.841
...so they compromised.


00:12:56.001 --> 00:13:01.441
<i>We agreed on a degree in natural
sciences, specialising in physics.</i>


00:13:02.521 --> 00:13:06.921
<i>The prevailing attitude at Oxford
at the time was very anti-work.</i>


00:13:07.961 --> 00:13:10.881
<i>You were supposed to be
brilliant without effort...</i>


00:13:11.001 --> 00:13:15.881
<i>or to accept your limitations
and get a fourth-class degree.</i>


00:13:17.081 --> 00:13:20.001
<i>To work hard to get
a better class of degree</i>


00:13:20.121 --> 00:13:22.841
<i>was regarded as the mark</i>
<i>of a "grey man"</i> -


00:13:22.961 --> 00:13:26.161
<i>the worst epithet
in the Oxford vocabulary.</i>


00:13:27.681 --> 00:13:28.801
I think Steve and I fell,


00:13:28.921 --> 00:13:31.001
both of us, right into that category,


00:13:31.121 --> 00:13:34.081
that there was no need to work
or to appear to work,


00:13:34.201 --> 00:13:37.361
and Steve was a very funny guy.


00:13:37.481 --> 00:13:40.801
He was able to appreciate jokes


00:13:40.921 --> 00:13:42.801
and tell jokes the whole time


00:13:42.921 --> 00:13:47.521
and spontaneous humour
was really his forte.


00:13:54.401 --> 00:13:57.001
Row! Row!


00:13:59.721 --> 00:14:01.281
Row!


00:14:01.401 --> 00:14:06.001
<i>At Oxford, I joined
the Rowing Club and became a cox.</i>


00:14:06.121 --> 00:14:09.241
<i>I relished in the freedom,
the speed</i>


00:14:09.361 --> 00:14:11.481
<i>and, of course,
calling the shots.</i>


00:14:17.881 --> 00:14:20.641
<i>The Rowing Club also introduced me</i>


00:14:20.761 --> 00:14:24.521
<i>to one of my favourite pastimes
at Oxford...</i>


00:14:28.801 --> 00:14:30.041
<i>...partying.</i>


00:14:42.321 --> 00:14:46.161
<i>I once calculated that I did
about a thousand hours' work</i>


00:14:46.281 --> 00:14:50.201
<i>in the three years I was there,
an average of an hour a day.</i>


00:14:52.081 --> 00:14:53.761
<i>I'm not proud of this lack of work,</i>


00:14:53.881 --> 00:14:57.721
<i>I'm just describing
my attitude at the time,</i>


00:14:57.841 --> 00:15:01.001
<i>which I shared
with most of my fellow students.</i>


00:15:03.881 --> 00:15:07.401
Within the whole year,
people gradually thought of Steve


00:15:07.521 --> 00:15:09.601
as being the brilliant guy in the year,


00:15:09.721 --> 00:15:12.521
but he was brilliant
in the sense that he could


00:15:12.641 --> 00:15:15.081
make off-the-cuff remarks
which were deep...


00:15:16.241 --> 00:15:20.241
...so he was definitely
a stand-out person of intellect.


00:15:20.361 --> 00:15:21.761
The question always was


00:15:21.881 --> 00:15:25.001
whether he would use that intellect
to go anywhere.


00:15:40.521 --> 00:15:42.481
Good evening, Professor Hawking.


00:15:45.801 --> 00:15:50.401
<i>Well, if the number
of champagne receptions one goes to</i>


00:15:50.521 --> 00:15:55.241
<i>is a measure of success,
then it would seem that I have made it.</i>


00:15:57.161 --> 00:15:58.681
<i>Tonight, I am guest of honour</i>


00:15:58.801 --> 00:16:03.801
<i>at the launch of a supercomputer
called Cosmos, in Cambridge.</i>


00:16:04.881 --> 00:16:09.201
<i>Cosmos is one of the most powerful
computers in the world</i>


00:16:09.321 --> 00:16:14.521
<i>and will enable us to better understand
our place in the universe.</i>


00:16:16.841 --> 00:16:18.601
Stephen's perhaps the world's


00:16:18.721 --> 00:16:23.961
most famous scientist, you know,
and one can't deny that it's fantastic


00:16:24.081 --> 00:16:25.521
to have his support.


00:16:25.641 --> 00:16:29.641
It really is a big day
for theoretical cosmology in the UK.


00:16:49.401 --> 00:16:53.401
<i>The notion of fame
is a curious thing to me.</i>


00:16:54.841 --> 00:16:59.121
<i>In my mind, I am a scientist
who has been lucky to work</i>


00:16:59.241 --> 00:17:02.361
<i>on some of the fundamental mysteries
of our universe.</i>


00:17:03.441 --> 00:17:08.721
<i>Sometimes I wonder if I am as famous
for my wheelchair and disabilities</i>


00:17:08.841 --> 00:17:10.881
<i>as I am for my discoveries.</i>


00:17:20.441 --> 00:17:22.961
<i>As my student days
were in full swing,</i>


00:17:23.081 --> 00:17:27.561
<i>I was gradually becoming aware
that all was not well.</i>


00:17:34.081 --> 00:17:37.761
<i>During my final year at Oxford,
I had noticed</i>


00:17:37.881 --> 00:17:40.321
<i>that I was getting rather clumsy</i>


00:17:40.441 --> 00:17:42.401
<i>in my movements</i>


00:17:42.521 --> 00:17:45.841
<i>and I fell over once or twice
for no apparent reason.</i>


00:17:49.241 --> 00:17:54.681
<i>But then one evening, late at night,
something more serious happened.</i>


00:17:54.801 --> 00:17:58.761
I recall the time
that Steve fell down the stairs.


00:17:58.881 --> 00:18:01.201
He fell downstairs all the way
to the bottom.


00:18:08.841 --> 00:18:12.201
He'd lost consciousness and then
he couldn't remember who he was.


00:18:14.961 --> 00:18:18.641
He couldn't remember where he was,
so it was a very serious thing.


00:18:31.041 --> 00:18:33.721
<i>When I look back at that fall,</i>


00:18:33.841 --> 00:18:38.641
<i>I didn't realise at the time it was
a warning sign of things to come...</i>


00:18:40.201 --> 00:18:45.521
<i>...but I recovered and soon had
more pressing things on my mind.</i>


00:18:48.121 --> 00:18:51.001
<i>Despite my relaxed attitude to study,</i>


00:18:51.121 --> 00:18:53.481
<i>I graduated with first-class honours</i>


00:18:53.601 --> 00:18:59.401
<i>and left Oxford for Cambridge University
to begin my PhD.</i>


00:19:03.801 --> 00:19:05.721
<i>Yet little did I know</i>


00:19:05.841 --> 00:19:09.481
<i>I would soon be diagnosed
with a crippling illness</i>


00:19:09.601 --> 00:19:12.121
<i>that would change my life forever.</i>


00:19:39.521 --> 00:19:45.641
Well, Stephen's speed of communication
has very gradually slowed down.


00:19:45.761 --> 00:19:48.721
A few years ago,
he was still able to use his hand switch


00:19:48.841 --> 00:19:50.281
and able to communicate by


00:19:50.401 --> 00:19:52.681
clicking this switch
on his wheelchair.


00:19:52.801 --> 00:19:54.681
When he wasn't able
to do that any more,


00:19:54.801 --> 00:19:58.201
we switched over to a switch
that he mounted to his cheek,


00:19:58.321 --> 00:19:59.921
but with him slowing down with that


00:20:00.041 --> 00:20:01.761
we've approached his sponsors


00:20:01.881 --> 00:20:04.841
and so they've been
looking into facial recognition.


00:20:06.001 --> 00:20:08.801
This is a high-speed camera


00:20:08.921 --> 00:20:11.681
which will allow us to see
very fine details


00:20:11.801 --> 00:20:14.241
on the facial expressions
and this will help us to improve


00:20:14.361 --> 00:20:17.241
- the rate of your speech and input.
- Yeah.


00:20:17.361 --> 00:20:21.841
<i>I have had to learn to live
with my slow rate of communication.</i>


00:20:21.961 --> 00:20:25.961
<i>I can only write by flinching
my cheek muscle</i>


00:20:26.081 --> 00:20:29.281
<i>to move the cursor on my computer.</i>


00:20:29.401 --> 00:20:32.241
<i>One day
I fear this muscle will fail,</i>


00:20:32.361 --> 00:20:35.881
<i>but I would like to be able
to speak more quickly.</i>


00:20:36.001 --> 00:20:39.361
Do you want me to change it
to English Alphabetic?


00:20:41.281 --> 00:20:43.161
Yeah. I'm buzzing.


00:20:44.681 --> 00:20:46.041
Yeah?


00:20:52.241 --> 00:20:54.521
Is that all...? Is that all OK, Stephen?


00:20:56.081 --> 00:20:57.001
Yeah?


00:20:58.161 --> 00:21:00.201
I'm not getting this at all,
Stephen.


00:21:00.321 --> 00:21:04.241
Sometimes he can make a mistake


00:21:04.361 --> 00:21:06.401
and then he deletes it and then


00:21:06.521 --> 00:21:08.481
he makes another mistake and deletes it


00:21:08.601 --> 00:21:10.641
and, you know, it can be frustrating
there watching


00:21:10.761 --> 00:21:13.521
and he can see the frustration
on his face as well.


00:21:16.241 --> 00:21:20.561
<i>I am hoping this current
generation of software experts</i>


00:21:20.681 --> 00:21:24.881
<i>can harness what little movement
I have left in my face</i>


00:21:25.001 --> 00:21:27.441
<i>and turn it into faster communication.</i>


00:21:28.641 --> 00:21:32.081
Your current piece of software
is a little dated, well, it's a lot dated,


00:21:32.201 --> 00:21:34.681
but you're very used to using it,


00:21:34.801 --> 00:21:36.641
so we've changed
the method by which


00:21:36.761 --> 00:21:39.201
your next word prediction works


00:21:39.321 --> 00:21:40.841
and it can pretty much pick up


00:21:40.961 --> 00:21:43.201
the correct word every single time,


00:21:43.321 --> 00:21:46.801
even if you're... even if
you're letters away from it.


00:21:46.921 --> 00:21:50.041
This is a big improvement
over the previous version.


00:21:50.161 --> 00:21:51.761
I really like it.


00:21:51.881 --> 00:21:55.641
- That's pretty amazing, the...
- I think it's actually gone brilliantly.


00:21:55.761 --> 00:21:58.961
The stuff that we've showed him
he was excited about.


00:21:59.081 --> 00:22:01.441
Anything better than
what he's doing,


00:22:01.561 --> 00:22:04.441
I think, is going to be a success
and anything


00:22:04.561 --> 00:22:08.681
that doesn't complicate Stephen's life
any more than it is,


00:22:08.801 --> 00:22:11.361
I think that will be a success as well.


00:22:11.481 --> 00:22:13.921
<i>ATV120,
thank you very much.</i>


00:22:22.241 --> 00:22:24.881
<i>In 1962, aged 20,</i>


00:22:25.001 --> 00:22:29.561
<i>when I arrived at Cambridge
to begin my PhD,</i>


00:22:29.681 --> 00:22:33.441
<i>I was also desperate for my voice
to be heard as I embarked</i>


00:22:33.561 --> 00:22:37.321
<i>on my first real
scientific challenge.</i>


00:22:37.441 --> 00:22:42.881
<i>At the time, two theories battled
to correctly describe the universe.</i>


00:22:44.161 --> 00:22:45.521
<i>The steady-state theory</i>


00:22:45.641 --> 00:22:47.681
<i>held that the universe
had always existed</i>


00:22:47.801 --> 00:22:50.201
<i>and would exist forever.</i>


00:22:51.801 --> 00:22:55.761
<i>But there was another,
more exciting idea.</i>


00:22:56.801 --> 00:22:58.481
<i>The big-bang theory suggested</i>


00:22:58.601 --> 00:23:02.081
<i>that the universe had begun
with a huge explosion.</i>


00:23:03.681 --> 00:23:05.281
<i>I decided to try to see</i>


00:23:05.401 --> 00:23:09.681
<i>if I could shed any light
on how the big bang came to be.</i>


00:23:12.921 --> 00:23:17.601
<i>But, by now, the immediate challenge
I was facing</i>


00:23:17.721 --> 00:23:20.321
<i>was to keep control of my body.</i>


00:23:20.441 --> 00:23:23.841
<i>My movements were becoming
even more erratic,</i>


00:23:23.961 --> 00:23:27.161
<i>though I was determined
not to worry my family,</i>


00:23:27.281 --> 00:23:29.761
<i>so I tried to keep it to myself.</i>


00:23:32.321 --> 00:23:35.521
Stephen went home for Christmas
after one term


00:23:35.641 --> 00:23:37.561
and the symptoms that he had


00:23:37.681 --> 00:23:41.081
had become too severe
to hide from his parents.


00:23:42.161 --> 00:23:46.401
His father insisted on taking him
to, I think, the family doctor first


00:23:46.521 --> 00:23:49.841
and then that doctor recommended
a specialist in London.


00:23:55.041 --> 00:23:57.841
So, after Christmas,
Stephen and his father went to London


00:23:57.961 --> 00:24:00.161
and St Bartholomew's Hospital.


00:24:02.921 --> 00:24:05.241
<i>I was in hospital for two weeks</i>


00:24:05.361 --> 00:24:08.121
<i>and had a wide range
of unpleasant tests.</i>


00:24:10.481 --> 00:24:15.721
<i>They took a muscle sample from my arm
and stuck electrodes into me.</i>


00:24:18.321 --> 00:24:23.361
<i>Then they injected some liquid
into my spine and took X-rays.</i>


00:24:24.881 --> 00:24:29.761
<i>Eventually, I was diagnosed with
amyotrophic lateral sclerosis...</i>


00:24:31.081 --> 00:24:34.601
<i>...otherwise known
as motor neurone disease or ALS.</i>


00:24:36.241 --> 00:24:37.921
<i>The prognosis was not good.</i>


00:24:39.121 --> 00:24:43.041
<i>I was given two to three years to live.</i>


00:24:46.081 --> 00:24:50.161
It was always hard to tell
how Stephen took it,


00:24:50.281 --> 00:24:52.561
because for one thing
he wouldn't talk about it.


00:24:54.001 --> 00:24:56.721
You know, if you can't do
anything about it, you...


00:24:56.841 --> 00:24:59.921
probably don't want
to talk about it, or...


00:25:01.321 --> 00:25:03.041
...or have people talk about it.


00:25:05.921 --> 00:25:08.441
He did seem pretty depressed


00:25:08.561 --> 00:25:11.121
and I don't think
he accepted it emotionally.


00:25:14.081 --> 00:25:15.961
The whole family went to pieces.


00:25:17.161 --> 00:25:19.801
It was such a shock for everybody.


00:25:19.921 --> 00:25:21.721
My aunt turned white in the night


00:25:21.841 --> 00:25:24.321
and we all thought he'd be, you know...


00:25:24.441 --> 00:25:26.961
he was not going to be able to live
for very long.


00:25:34.481 --> 00:25:37.161
<i>Not knowing
what was going to happen to me</i>


00:25:37.281 --> 00:25:40.441
<i>or how rapidly the disease would progress,</i>


00:25:40.561 --> 00:25:41.921
<i>I was at a loose end.</i>


00:25:43.241 --> 00:25:47.001
<i>The doctors told me to go back to
Cambridge...


00:25:47.002 --> 00:25:49.802
<i>and carry on with my research...</i>


00:25:50.361 --> 00:25:54.721
<i>...but I was not making much progress
and anyway</i>


00:25:54.841 --> 00:25:57.921
<i>I might not live long enough
to finish my PhD.</i>


00:26:00.161 --> 00:26:03.041
<i>I felt somewhat
of a tragic character,</i>


00:26:03.161 --> 00:26:05.721
<i>I took to listening to Wagner.</i>


00:27:03.441 --> 00:27:07.841
<i>Wagner's</i> The Annunciation Of Death
<i>made a great impression on me</i>


00:27:07.961 --> 00:27:12.961
<i>when I first heard it, as I had just
developed motor neurone disease.</i>


00:27:15.681 --> 00:27:19.521
<i>I identified with it
and still do today.</i>


00:27:36.681 --> 00:27:41.241
<i>But as it turned out, I didn't die.</i>


00:27:41.361 --> 00:27:45.361
<i>At first, the disease seemed
to progress fairly rapidly.</i>


00:27:45.481 --> 00:27:49.801
<i>As time went by, however,
it seemed to slow down.</i>


00:27:51.121 --> 00:27:53.681
<i>I also began to make progress
with my work.</i>


00:27:55.321 --> 00:27:57.641
<i>But what really made the difference</i>


00:27:57.761 --> 00:28:01.921
<i>was falling in love with a girl
called Jane Wilde</i>


00:28:02.041 --> 00:28:05.561
<i>whom I had met
about the same time I was diagnosed.</i>


00:28:05.681 --> 00:28:08.441
<i>This gave me something to live for.</i>


00:28:11.681 --> 00:28:14.201
Oh, he was great fun, he was eccentric.


00:28:15.281 --> 00:28:19.681
I was really drawn
to his very wide smile


00:28:19.801 --> 00:28:22.001
and his beautiful grey eyes...


00:28:23.361 --> 00:28:26.321
...and I think that's what made me
fall in love with him.


00:28:28.561 --> 00:28:30.721
We were going to defy the disease,


00:28:30.841 --> 00:28:32.921
we were going to defy the doctors


00:28:33.041 --> 00:28:36.001
and we were going
to challenge the future.


00:28:37.201 --> 00:28:40.841
<i>Jane was beautiful and gentle</i>


00:28:40.961 --> 00:28:44.641
<i>and seemingly undaunted
by the harsh reality of my illness.</i>


00:28:55.881 --> 00:28:59.041
<i>Falling in love
and getting engaged</i>


00:28:59.161 --> 00:29:01.761
<i>was the motivation that I needed.</i>


00:29:01.881 --> 00:29:04.761
<i>If I were to get married,
I had to get a job,</i>


00:29:04.881 --> 00:29:08.961
<i>and to get a job
I had to finish my PhD.</i>


00:29:09.081 --> 00:29:13.761
<i>I therefore started working hard
for the first time in my life.</i>


00:29:15.001 --> 00:29:18.361
<i>To my surprise, I found I liked it.</i>


00:29:18.481 --> 00:29:23.481
Well, Stephen had found
his groove, he'd found his trajectory


00:29:23.601 --> 00:29:26.281
and when Stephen finds a trajectory
he pursues it.


00:29:27.881 --> 00:29:31.601
It was terribly exciting
because he had been so depressed


00:29:31.721 --> 00:29:35.641
and here he was
with a new lease of life.


00:29:37.201 --> 00:29:40.361
<i>Perhaps because I realised
I might not have much time,</i>


00:29:40.481 --> 00:29:42.321
<i>I renewed my efforts</i>


00:29:42.441 --> 00:29:47.321
<i>to tackle the big question in cosmology
in the early '60s...</i>


00:29:48.361 --> 00:29:51.841
<i>...did the universe
have a beginning or not?</i>


00:29:51.961 --> 00:29:57.001
<i>Many scientists were instinctively
opposed to the idea of a big bang,</i>


00:29:57.121 --> 00:30:00.601
<i>because it implies a moment of creation...</i>


00:30:02.401 --> 00:30:03.601
<i>...the hand of God.</i>


00:30:04.801 --> 00:30:09.161
<i>For me, though, religion has
no role to play in physics,</i>


00:30:09.281 --> 00:30:12.961
<i>so I wondered if the big bang
could have happened on its own</i>


00:30:13.081 --> 00:30:15.321
<i>without the need for a god
to get it going?</i>


00:30:19.881 --> 00:30:22.401
<i>The key was in the theory
of black holes.</i>


00:30:24.121 --> 00:30:29.001
<i>At the time, physicist Roger Penrose
was working on what happens</i>


00:30:29.121 --> 00:30:32.521
<i>when a star collapses
under the force of its own gravity.</i>


00:30:34.081 --> 00:30:36.921
<i>Penrose claimed
the star would crush itself</i>


00:30:37.041 --> 00:30:39.281
<i>to a tiny point of infinite density</i>


00:30:39.401 --> 00:30:42.321
<i>where even time itself
would come to a stop.</i>


00:30:43.721 --> 00:30:48.161
<i>He called it a "singularity" -
the heart of a black hole.</i>


00:30:49.721 --> 00:30:54.041
When I was doing this work
on gravitation collapse, black holes,


00:30:54.161 --> 00:30:55.641
I'd never heard of Stephen,


00:30:55.761 --> 00:30:58.761
so I had a little private session,
and he was somebody


00:30:58.881 --> 00:31:01.121
who picked up ideas quickly.


00:31:01.241 --> 00:31:02.921
He sort of stood out
as being someone


00:31:03.041 --> 00:31:06.081
who asked extremely awkward
questions. I do remember that!


00:31:07.841 --> 00:31:09.601
<i>I worked relentlessly to see</i>


00:31:09.721 --> 00:31:14.521
<i>if I could apply the notion
of a singularity to the entire universe.</i>


00:31:15.641 --> 00:31:17.961
<i>Then, suddenly, I had it.</i>


00:31:21.081 --> 00:31:24.001
<i>I imagined going backwards
to the beginning</i>


00:31:24.121 --> 00:31:26.561
<i>and worked out that,
right at the start,</i>


00:31:26.681 --> 00:31:29.881
<i>the universe
would have been a singularity too.</i>


00:31:33.161 --> 00:31:37.761
<i>Here, time stops - you've reached
the true beginning of everything.</i>


00:31:39.441 --> 00:31:44.041
<i>There is no previous time in which
the universe could have had a cause.</i>


00:31:45.201 --> 00:31:49.881
<i>It spontaneously created itself
in the big bang.</i>


00:31:58.361 --> 00:32:02.361
The work that Stephen did
basically showed there was


00:32:02.481 --> 00:32:05.441
a singular state
which couldn't have come


00:32:05.561 --> 00:32:08.521
from a previous universe.


00:32:08.641 --> 00:32:13.321
So you could say it is a theory which
tells you the universe had a beginning.


00:32:19.041 --> 00:32:21.521
<i>By addressing
the question of creation</i>


00:32:21.641 --> 00:32:23.001
<i>that physicists had avoided,</i>


00:32:23.121 --> 00:32:27.841
<i>I had controversially shown
the laws of nature suggest</i>


00:32:27.961 --> 00:32:31.081
<i>there is no need for a creator or a god.</i>


00:32:32.401 --> 00:32:36.401
<i>The universe just
came into existence all by itself.</i>


00:32:47.561 --> 00:32:51.321
<i>My findings about the big bang
and the possible beginning</i>


00:32:51.441 --> 00:32:56.281
<i>of the universe gave me the results
I needed to enhance my PhD.</i>


00:32:57.361 --> 00:32:59.761
<i>I applied for a research fellowship</i>


00:32:59.881 --> 00:33:03.721
<i>at Gonville and Caius College
which proved successful.</i>


00:33:03.841 --> 00:33:07.681
<i>The money from the fellowship meant
that Jane and I could get married,</i>


00:33:07.801 --> 00:33:11.481
<i>which we did in July 1965.</i>


00:33:14.841 --> 00:33:20.281
We had the maximum
number of guests in the tiny chapel.


00:33:20.401 --> 00:33:25.841
Stephen was walking with a stick
and he was losing strength in his arms


00:33:25.961 --> 00:33:30.161
and we went off for our honeymoon
to upstate New York


00:33:30.281 --> 00:33:34.041
to a physics conference
at Cornell University


00:33:34.161 --> 00:33:39.241
and there I got to know
that a goddess in Stephen's life,


00:33:39.361 --> 00:33:42.561
with whom I was sharing the marriage,
was physics!


00:33:47.841 --> 00:33:50.641
<i>After returning
from our honeymoon,</i>


00:33:50.761 --> 00:33:51.961
<i>Jane and I bought a house</i>


00:33:52.081 --> 00:33:54.881
<i>in Little St Mary's Lane
in the centre of Cambridge.</i>


00:33:58.441 --> 00:34:03.001
<i>In March 1966,
I completed and submitted</i>


00:34:03.121 --> 00:34:06.041
<i>my PhD thesis,
with the help of Jane,</i>


00:34:06.161 --> 00:34:10.881
<i>who had spent many painstaking hours
at the typewriter, typing it up.</i>


00:34:12.801 --> 00:34:17.801
<i>The findings in my thesis greatly
enhanced my reputation in cosmology.</i>


00:34:19.321 --> 00:34:23.921
Stephen's profile
certainly did, I think, rocket.


00:34:24.041 --> 00:34:28.721
It's important to know
that there was a big bang


00:34:28.841 --> 00:34:33.001
because that governs our cosmology.


00:34:34.801 --> 00:34:38.121
It's also important to know
what it was like


00:34:38.241 --> 00:34:40.681
and why it was like what it was like,


00:34:40.801 --> 00:34:44.001
because when you trace back
everything in nature


00:34:44.121 --> 00:34:46.561
you get ultimately to the big bang,


00:34:46.681 --> 00:34:49.121
and the fact that it was
a singular state


00:34:49.241 --> 00:34:52.921
is very much part of that understanding.


00:35:10.081 --> 00:35:16.321
Stephen, I'll just do a quick recap, um...
of my PhD, yeah? That's fine, yeah?


00:35:16.441 --> 00:35:18.641
<i>My early work went some way
to answering</i>


00:35:18.761 --> 00:35:21.161
<i>how our universe began,</i>


00:35:21.281 --> 00:35:25.201
<i>but there is still
plenty more to find out.</i>


00:35:25.321 --> 00:35:30.401
Brane-world black holes should radiate
if they aren't extreme, but slowly.


00:35:30.521 --> 00:35:36.041
<i>At Cambridge, I mentor a new generation
of cosmologists</i>


00:35:36.161 --> 00:35:39.681
<i>who are tackling ever-tougher questions.</i>


00:35:39.801 --> 00:35:42.641
<i>It's this work that I enjoy the most.</i>


00:35:42.761 --> 00:35:46.001
You might as well say
the Schwarzschild solution doesn't exist


00:35:46.121 --> 00:35:47.481
because it radiates.


00:35:48.561 --> 00:35:50.761
Well, I'll have to think about it.


00:35:50.881 --> 00:35:53.441
He's in the office every day
and, I mean, it's basically


00:35:53.561 --> 00:35:56.161
for us PhD students.
It's great - I mean, he's there for us


00:35:56.281 --> 00:36:01.401
if we want him to, kind of, right, so, no,
no, I mean, it's... it's a great privilege.


00:36:04.521 --> 00:36:09.161
<i>I have always wanted to share
my enthusiasm and excitement.</i>


00:36:10.681 --> 00:36:13.441
<i>There's nothing like
the eureka moment</i>


00:36:13.561 --> 00:36:16.841
<i>of discovering something
that no-one knew before.</i>


00:36:16.961 --> 00:36:21.121
<i>I won't compare it to sex,
but it lasts longer.</i>


00:36:30.681 --> 00:36:33.241
<i>My next major eureka moment
happened</i>


00:36:33.361 --> 00:36:36.081
<i>when I was least expecting it.</i>


00:36:36.201 --> 00:36:39.641
<i>In the autumn of 1970,</i>


00:36:39.761 --> 00:36:43.561
<i>I had been concentrating
my research on black holes.</i>


00:36:45.921 --> 00:36:49.961
<i>By now, Jane and I had two children -</i>


00:36:50.081 --> 00:36:54.401
<i>Robert, aged three years,
and Lucy, who had just been born.</i>


00:36:54.521 --> 00:36:59.001
<i>One evening in November,
as I was getting ready for bed,</i>


00:36:59.121 --> 00:37:02.681
<i>an idea charged through my brain.</i>


00:37:04.561 --> 00:37:07.601
<i>Only cosmologists would truly grasp it,</i>


00:37:07.721 --> 00:37:10.281
<i>but it would greatly enhance
my reputation.</i>


00:37:12.441 --> 00:37:14.561
I was sitting
on one side of the bed


00:37:14.681 --> 00:37:16.841
and Stephen was sitting
on the other side of the bed


00:37:16.961 --> 00:37:18.441
tussling with his buttons


00:37:18.561 --> 00:37:24.441
and this was one evening
when he took longer about it than usual.


00:37:24.561 --> 00:37:26.681
So I finished feeding the baby


00:37:26.801 --> 00:37:28.761
and then he said...


00:37:30.161 --> 00:37:32.481
"I think I've solved a problem."


00:37:35.401 --> 00:37:39.241
<i>Suddenly, I had a revelation
about what happens</i>


00:37:39.361 --> 00:37:42.281
<i>when two black holes collide and merge.</i>


00:37:44.361 --> 00:37:47.601
<i>I realised that the surface area
of the new black hole</i>


00:37:47.721 --> 00:37:51.241
<i>could only get bigger -
it could never decrease in size.</i>


00:37:52.321 --> 00:37:54.961
<i>This may sound like
an obscure discovery,</i>


00:37:55.081 --> 00:37:58.881
<i>but it revealed some fundamental
properties of the universe.</i>


00:38:00.001 --> 00:38:03.281
<i>Even though few physicists
could understand it at the time,</i>


00:38:03.401 --> 00:38:05.801
<i>for me, it was very exciting.</i>


00:38:07.161 --> 00:38:11.121
<i>I was writing the rule book
for black holes.</i>


00:38:11.241 --> 00:38:14.081
<i>Now my voice would really be heard.</i>


00:38:16.561 --> 00:38:19.201
I knew he'd done something
very important,


00:38:19.321 --> 00:38:21.121
so I was absolutely thrilled


00:38:21.241 --> 00:38:24.601
and this, of course, made his name
in a big way in physics -


00:38:24.721 --> 00:38:29.041
he'd been recognised
as somebody with great potential,


00:38:29.161 --> 00:38:32.961
but now he really had
a discovery to his name.


00:38:51.761 --> 00:38:55.721
<i>By the early 1970s,
my career in cosmology</i>


00:38:55.841 --> 00:39:00.001
<i>and my understandings of the universe
were beginning to blossom.</i>


00:39:03.841 --> 00:39:06.361
<i>But as my mind grew in confidence</i>


00:39:06.481 --> 00:39:09.521
<i>my body was going
into a rapid decline.</i>


00:39:14.321 --> 00:39:18.881
<i>At home, I was reluctant
to ask for help from outsiders</i>


00:39:19.001 --> 00:39:22.041
<i>and was relying upon Jane
more and more to help me</i>


00:39:22.161 --> 00:39:26.441
<i>get up in the morning,
get dressed and get to work.</i>


00:39:26.561 --> 00:39:32.441
<i>And when I got to Cambridge
I needed my students to help me too.</i>


00:39:32.561 --> 00:39:34.801
Are we signing this?


00:39:34.921 --> 00:39:36.081
Yes.


00:39:36.201 --> 00:39:37.721
I first met Stephen


00:39:37.841 --> 00:39:42.841
in 1972 at Cambridge
when I became his PhD student.


00:39:42.961 --> 00:39:46.721
He was in a wheelchair,
but a push wheelchair,


00:39:46.841 --> 00:39:50.881
and so I also had a role in helping him -
with eating


00:39:51.001 --> 00:39:54.401
and moving around and having coffee
and tea and things like that,


00:39:54.521 --> 00:39:57.721
so it was a rather unusual relationship.


00:39:57.841 --> 00:40:01.961
In those days, Stephen, of course,
was still speaking with his voice,


00:40:02.081 --> 00:40:03.761
but his voice was still weak,


00:40:03.881 --> 00:40:06.121
so it wasn't so easy to hear
what he was saying.


00:40:22.721 --> 00:40:24.601
And, indeed,
when I travelled with Stephen,


00:40:24.721 --> 00:40:27.961
I would often be acting
as an interpreter,


00:40:28.081 --> 00:40:29.481
so Stephen would say something


00:40:29.601 --> 00:40:32.361
and if the person he was speaking to
couldn't understand,


00:40:32.481 --> 00:40:33.881
I would then repeat it.


00:40:43.681 --> 00:40:46.521
<i>Although I was becoming
increasingly trapped</i>


00:40:46.641 --> 00:40:52.281
<i>inside my dysfunctioning body,
fortunately my mind was unaffected.</i>


00:40:55.321 --> 00:41:00.801
<i>I had a new big question
I felt compelled to find the answer to.</i>


00:41:00.921 --> 00:41:05.241
<i>My reputation in the field of black holes
was established</i>


00:41:05.361 --> 00:41:07.401
<i>as we entered a golden age.</i>


00:41:08.961 --> 00:41:11.361
<i>But my next discovery
would throw</i>


00:41:11.481 --> 00:41:15.321
<i>all of cosmologists' findings to date
up in the air.</i>


00:41:16.361 --> 00:41:19.561
<i>The calculations I was working on</i>


00:41:19.681 --> 00:41:23.841
<i>involved what happened to particles
on the edge of a black hole</i>


00:41:23.961 --> 00:41:26.961
<i>that were sucked in and disappeared.</i>


00:41:28.321 --> 00:41:30.121
<i>To my great surprise,</i>


00:41:30.241 --> 00:41:33.801
<i>I found that some particles
could escape the black hole,</i>


00:41:33.921 --> 00:41:37.841
<i>which seemed to make a mockery
of the known laws of physics.</i>


00:41:40.521 --> 00:41:42.761
<i>At first, I thought
this must be a mistake.</i>


00:41:42.881 --> 00:41:46.241
I do remember
when he was working on this problem


00:41:46.361 --> 00:41:49.441
and, indeed, I even remember
when he told me he was working out


00:41:49.561 --> 00:41:52.241
the quantum effects of these black holes
and he seemed to be getting


00:41:52.361 --> 00:41:54.801
this flux of particles coming out.


00:41:54.921 --> 00:41:56.681
When Stephen's
thinking about a problem


00:41:56.801 --> 00:41:59.201
he will become
obviously obsessed with it.


00:42:00.721 --> 00:42:03.001
It was a very intense period.


00:42:03.121 --> 00:42:06.641
It was when he could be surrounded
by children


00:42:06.761 --> 00:42:12.121
and not notice what was going on,
because he was like Rodin's Thinker


00:42:12.241 --> 00:42:14.641
with his head in his hands,


00:42:14.761 --> 00:42:19.561
often accompanied by Wagner
blaring out from the loudspeakers.


00:42:36.921 --> 00:42:38.681
It used to drive me spare!


00:42:44.441 --> 00:42:47.801
<i>Finally, after months
of exhaustive work,</i>


00:42:47.921 --> 00:42:49.801
<i>I found what I was looking for.</i>


00:42:49.921 --> 00:42:54.961
<i>Contrary to all previously held theories
on black holes,</i>


00:42:55.081 --> 00:42:58.161
<i>I discovered
that they must emit particles,</i>


00:42:58.281 --> 00:43:01.201
<i>like a hot body losing heat.</i>


00:43:01.321 --> 00:43:03.641
<i>This "evaporation" meant, in theory,</i>


00:43:03.761 --> 00:43:06.361
<i>a black hole could eventually
disappear.</i>


00:43:12.601 --> 00:43:18.281
<i>I announced my findings
on St Valentine's Day in 1974</i>


00:43:18.401 --> 00:43:23.721
<i>at a cosmology conference in Oxford,
to a packed audience.</i>


00:43:23.841 --> 00:43:25.081
He came to an end.


00:43:26.401 --> 00:43:30.121
And there was absolute silence
in the lecture hall...


00:43:31.721 --> 00:43:36.561
...and I can see it now -
the chairman of the lecture


00:43:36.681 --> 00:43:40.121
jumped to his feet
and instead of saying,


00:43:40.241 --> 00:43:44.081
"Oh, I must thank Professor Hawking
for his remarkable lecture,"


00:43:44.201 --> 00:43:47.961
he said, "This is preposterous!
I have never heard anything like it!"


00:43:50.161 --> 00:43:52.521
The whole place was abuzz.


00:43:52.641 --> 00:43:55.881
People couldn't believe
what they had heard.


00:43:57.681 --> 00:43:59.921
<i>My controversial discovery</i>


00:44:00.041 --> 00:44:02.321
<i>initially shocked
the world of physics...</i>


00:44:03.721 --> 00:44:08.681
<i>...but eventually it became accepted
and known as Hawking radiation.</i>


00:44:10.441 --> 00:44:12.521
<i>I am proud to have discovered it.</i>


00:44:14.241 --> 00:44:16.581
This was a
remarkably important result


00:44:16.582 --> 00:44:18.482
because it was a result which...


00:44:18.601 --> 00:44:24.881
unified relativity theory and
quantum theory and thermodynamics.


00:44:25.001 --> 00:44:30.081
And physics is really
all about unifying ideas.


00:44:30.201 --> 00:44:32.881
These three subjects
seemed to be brought together


00:44:33.001 --> 00:44:36.681
and this was the first time we'd seen
that kind of unification.


00:44:36.801 --> 00:44:40.281
Every now and then in physics,
you get a result which is...


00:44:40.401 --> 00:44:43.361
it's so beautiful, it really is
like rolling candy on the tongue.


00:44:43.481 --> 00:44:46.361
Oh, I was enormously proud...


00:44:46.481 --> 00:44:49.521
enormously proud
of what Stephen had achieved.


00:44:50.921 --> 00:44:54.241
<i>After I announced my theory
of Hawking radiation</i>


00:44:54.361 --> 00:44:58.161
<i>and a later discovery
of exploding black holes,</i>


00:44:58.281 --> 00:45:01.441
<i>a procession of international awards
followed.</i>


00:45:01.561 --> 00:45:07.001
<i>In the spring of 1974,
I was inducted into the Royal Society,</i>


00:45:07.121 --> 00:45:09.841
<i>one of the most prestigious
bodies of scientists.</i>


00:45:11.161 --> 00:45:14.441
<i>My name now sat alongside
Isaac Newton and Charles Darwin.</i>


00:45:16.561 --> 00:45:20.081
<i>A year later, I received
the Gold Medal for Science,</i>


00:45:20.201 --> 00:45:23.361
<i>from Pope Paul VI.</i>


00:45:23.481 --> 00:45:29.241
<i>Aged 32, I was thrilled to have
such high-profile awards to my name</i>


00:45:29.361 --> 00:45:32.281
<i>and exciting new opportunities beckoned.</i>


00:45:34.201 --> 00:45:38.321
<i>- # All the leaves are brown
- # All the leaves are brown</i>


00:45:38.441 --> 00:45:42.521
<i>- # And the sky is grey
- # And the sky is grey</i>


00:45:42.641 --> 00:45:46.641
- # <i>I've been for a walk
- # I've been for a walk</i>


00:45:46.761 --> 00:45:50.641
- # <i>on a winter's day
- # on a winter's day</i>


00:45:50.761 --> 00:45:54.561
- # <i>I'd be safe and warm
- # I'd be safe and warm</i>


00:45:54.681 --> 00:45:59.921
- <i># if was in LA</i>
- <i># if was in LA... #</i>


00:46:00.041 --> 00:46:04.601
<i>In 1974,
doors started to open for me...</i>


00:46:07.561 --> 00:46:10.081
<i>...and I was invited
on a visiting professorship</i>


00:46:10.201 --> 00:46:14.841
<i>to the California Institute of Technology
in Los Angeles.</i>


00:46:16.041 --> 00:46:19.921
<i>This meant moving with the whole family
for the academic year</i>


00:46:20.041 --> 00:46:23.561
<i>and the lure of the American West Coast
was irresistible.</i>


00:46:32.401 --> 00:46:34.561
We were in the midst
of a revolution


00:46:34.681 --> 00:46:37.521
in the application of quantum physics
to black holes.


00:46:37.641 --> 00:46:40.001
I was simply hoping
to have a year in which


00:46:40.121 --> 00:46:43.761
the result would be better science
done by Caltech scientists,


00:46:43.881 --> 00:46:45.641
by my research group and others


00:46:45.761 --> 00:46:47.801
and better science done by Stephen.


00:46:49.241 --> 00:46:53.761
<i>Life in California was
very different from Cambridge.</i>


00:46:53.881 --> 00:46:56.561
<i>I now had a large salary
and a big house</i>


00:46:56.681 --> 00:46:59.841
<i>just a stone's throw from the university.</i>


00:47:01.681 --> 00:47:04.041
Well, America could have been
on another planet.


00:47:04.161 --> 00:47:05.841
We were living in the lap of luxury,


00:47:05.961 --> 00:47:09.041
no expense spared to make us welcome,


00:47:09.161 --> 00:47:13.041
and this great gift
of an electric wheelchair


00:47:13.161 --> 00:47:15.881
provided for Stephen's use.


00:47:16.001 --> 00:47:21.241
<i>Yet, although we had all
the mod cons that America could offer us,</i>


00:47:21.361 --> 00:47:26.001
<i>it was becoming clear that the demands
of home life on Jane</i>


00:47:26.121 --> 00:47:28.081
<i>were becoming too intense.</i>


00:47:30.801 --> 00:47:33.481
<i>Looking after two young children</i>


00:47:33.601 --> 00:47:37.121
<i>and trying to cope
with my ever-increasing disabilities</i>


00:47:37.241 --> 00:47:39.481
<i>were causing too much strain.</i>


00:47:41.161 --> 00:47:45.881
I could not see my way through


00:47:46.001 --> 00:47:48.561
what it was going to demand of me...


00:47:49.721 --> 00:47:51.241
...and this wonderful idea came to me -


00:47:51.361 --> 00:47:54.001
if we were going
to go to California


00:47:54.121 --> 00:47:56.481
and the students were
going to come too,


00:47:56.601 --> 00:48:00.321
why didn't we offer them
a bed in our house


00:48:00.441 --> 00:48:02.761
in return for some help
with Stephen?


00:48:03.921 --> 00:48:06.201
Of course,
it's not the normal relationship


00:48:06.321 --> 00:48:09.521
between a student
and the PhD supervisor,


00:48:09.641 --> 00:48:13.481
but, for me, that was a good deal
because I lived rent free


00:48:13.601 --> 00:48:17.121
in exchange for sort of helping
Stephen out around the home,


00:48:17.241 --> 00:48:20.841
you know, at bath times and helping
with meals and things like that.


00:48:23.041 --> 00:48:26.641
<i>But the excitement
of a new life in California</i>


00:48:26.761 --> 00:48:30.361
<i>was harshly interrupted
by my motor neurone disease.</i>


00:48:31.441 --> 00:48:35.321
<i>The physical symptoms took
an irreversible turn for the worse.</i>


00:48:37.281 --> 00:48:39.801
That was the year in which
he lost the use of his hands.


00:48:39.921 --> 00:48:42.841
When he arrived,
he could still write equations,


00:48:42.961 --> 00:48:44.281
though with some difficulty.


00:48:44.401 --> 00:48:45.881
By the end of the year, he couldn't.


00:48:46.001 --> 00:48:49.121
So it was... in terms of
his physical development,


00:48:49.241 --> 00:48:51.201
it was a more difficult period.


00:48:51.321 --> 00:48:53.641
On the other hand,
in terms of his mental development,


00:48:53.761 --> 00:48:56.601
as he gradually lost
the use of his hands,


00:48:56.721 --> 00:49:00.521
he further developed
his unique ways of thinking


00:49:00.641 --> 00:49:04.921
by manipulating shapes
and topologies in his head


00:49:05.041 --> 00:49:09.281
and became even more advanced
than the rest of us.


00:49:12.401 --> 00:49:15.801
<i>By losing
the finer dexterity of my hands,</i>


00:49:15.921 --> 00:49:19.161
<i>I was forced to travel through
the universe in my mind</i>


00:49:19.281 --> 00:49:22.641
<i>and try to visualise
the ways in which it worked.</i>


00:49:25.001 --> 00:49:28.801
He could move at lightning speed
across the frontiers of knowledge


00:49:28.921 --> 00:49:31.561
and see things
that nobody else could see.


00:49:31.681 --> 00:49:39.321
The disability forced him to carry himself
in new ways, new directions.


00:49:57.161 --> 00:50:01.361
<i>Turning problems over
in my mind has been my main method</i>


00:50:01.481 --> 00:50:04.041
<i>of discovery for nearly half my life now.</i>


00:50:06.121 --> 00:50:09.201
<i>While all around me
people have buzzed away,</i>


00:50:09.321 --> 00:50:10.841
<i>deep in conversation,</i>


00:50:10.961 --> 00:50:13.441
<i>I have often been transported afar,</i>


00:50:13.561 --> 00:50:19.761
<i>lost inside my own thoughts, trying
to fathom how the universe works.</i>


00:51:04.761 --> 00:51:09.761
<i>When we returned from California
in the summer of 1975,</i>


00:51:09.881 --> 00:51:13.961
<i>much of the future,
living with my illness, seemed uncertain.</i>


00:51:15.361 --> 00:51:20.521
<i>So Jane and I took comfort
in the security of a happy family life...</i>


00:51:22.481 --> 00:51:24.201
<i>...and, to make home life easier,</i>


00:51:24.321 --> 00:51:27.041
<i>we realised my disabilities
meant that we needed</i>


00:51:27.161 --> 00:51:29.801
<i>a student living with us full-time.</i>


00:51:30.841 --> 00:51:32.561
I knew that Stephen Hawking was
a brilliant scientist


00:51:32.681 --> 00:51:34.201
and I was told that he was
severely disabled


00:51:34.321 --> 00:51:36.161
and I was told that his voice
was very weak,


00:51:36.281 --> 00:51:37.761
so it would probably take about a week


00:51:37.881 --> 00:51:40.201
before, you know, I could understand
what he was saying.


00:51:40.321 --> 00:51:43.641
Of course,
because of Stephen's circumstances,


00:51:43.761 --> 00:51:46.601
you could say
no day was really normal.


00:51:48.161 --> 00:51:52.081
Stephen had these massive batteries
that... in his wheelchair


00:51:52.201 --> 00:51:54.281
and, of course, he had spares,
and so I loaded as many as I could


00:51:54.401 --> 00:51:56.201
on the bottom
of Stephen's wheelchair


00:51:56.321 --> 00:51:58.801
and Stephen didn't know
that I had loaded it down so heavily


00:51:58.921 --> 00:52:01.081
and I didn't want to have to carry
the thing, so he starts up the hill


00:52:01.201 --> 00:52:03.241
and then I see him turning the corner
and he's about...


00:52:03.361 --> 00:52:07.201
10... about 20 metres away
and I see him slowly tipping backwards


00:52:07.321 --> 00:52:10.681
and he fell over into the bushes.


00:52:13.841 --> 00:52:17.761
Yeah, it was somewhat alarming to see
the master of gravity being overcome


00:52:17.881 --> 00:52:22.201
by the Earth's gravitational field
and falling into the... into the bushes.


00:52:22.321 --> 00:52:25.001
I went up to retrieve him
and he was, of course, quite upset


00:52:25.121 --> 00:52:26.161
and it was perhaps good


00:52:26.281 --> 00:52:28.841
that I couldn't understand
all that he said on that occasion.


00:52:30.881 --> 00:52:34.121
I could have caught that
if I realised it was gonna stop.


00:52:35.201 --> 00:52:36.841
<i>Although
my increasing disabilities</i>


00:52:36.961 --> 00:52:40.481
<i>were greatly affecting my life
more and more,</i>


00:52:40.601 --> 00:52:44.361
<i>I was fiercely reluctant
to accept nursing care.</i>


00:52:47.801 --> 00:52:51.841
<i>I was convinced that I could build
a team of people around me</i>


00:52:51.961 --> 00:52:54.561
<i>who could care for me
in their own way.</i>


00:52:54.681 --> 00:52:56.201
We didn't have any nurses


00:52:56.321 --> 00:53:01.281
at all in the department - that was part
of my role. I would look after him.


00:53:01.401 --> 00:53:03.961
- You'd like to reply to that one?
- Mm-hm.


00:53:04.081 --> 00:53:05.801
Well, I had to wipe his nose,


00:53:05.921 --> 00:53:11.081
comb his hair for him
if it was falling down into his eyes


00:53:11.201 --> 00:53:15.001
and I could see that when he was eating
or drinking


00:53:15.121 --> 00:53:20.841
this could cause a problem
and a very big one for me


00:53:20.961 --> 00:53:24.641
because I didn't have
any nursing training whatsoever.


00:53:27.041 --> 00:53:29.961
He would have these coughing fits
that would be quite severe


00:53:30.081 --> 00:53:33.121
and, you know, you'd sort of think he
was gonna choke and die at any moment.


00:53:34.481 --> 00:53:37.881
I felt responsible to try to give him
the best care that I could,


00:53:38.001 --> 00:53:40.841
but it was scary thinking
what might happen to him.


00:53:44.561 --> 00:53:49.881
One night Stephen had
the most horrendous choking fit


00:53:50.001 --> 00:53:52.161
and I just didn't know what to do


00:53:52.281 --> 00:53:53.801
and everything just shook,


00:53:53.921 --> 00:53:56.041
windows rattled, doors shook.


00:53:56.161 --> 00:53:59.761
It was the most terrifying experience


00:53:59.881 --> 00:54:01.681
and it could have been critical.


00:54:04.041 --> 00:54:07.681
<i>Although I was able to live
and work as I wanted,</i>


00:54:07.801 --> 00:54:10.321
<i>I was never really able to understand</i>


00:54:10.441 --> 00:54:13.921
<i>the strain it was placing
on the people around me...</i>


00:54:15.201 --> 00:54:17.561
<i>...especially my wife, Jane.</i>


00:54:20.481 --> 00:54:25.201
I was beginning to feel that there
were two faces to our situation...


00:54:28.321 --> 00:54:35.321
...one was the public image,
the wunderkind of physics, um...


00:54:35.441 --> 00:54:39.641
who had overcome motor neurone disease,


00:54:39.761 --> 00:54:43.161
who was whizzing round the world
in his wheelchair


00:54:43.281 --> 00:54:45.801
to receive honours and medals,


00:54:45.921 --> 00:54:51.681
and the other side, the other face,
was the home situation,


00:54:51.801 --> 00:54:57.081
where sometimes the illness forced us
into our own little black hole.


00:55:10.281 --> 00:55:14.041
<i>The decline in my health
was a stark reminder</i>


00:55:14.042 --> 00:55:16.442
<i>that time was against me.</i>


00:55:16.561 --> 00:55:20.161
<i>Yet despite the pressures
on my family</i>


00:55:20.281 --> 00:55:23.721
<i>I was determined to realise
a lifelong ambition</i>


00:55:23.841 --> 00:55:27.801
<i>by writing a popular book
about how the universe had begun.</i>


00:55:29.121 --> 00:55:31.201
<i>I wanted the book to be read</i>


00:55:31.321 --> 00:55:36.441
<i>by millions of people around the world,
like a bestselling airport novel.</i>


00:55:37.761 --> 00:55:39.281
I did not think it would work.


00:55:39.401 --> 00:55:42.401
I did not think it would work
because basically


00:55:42.521 --> 00:55:45.401
if you look at all the other
books in airports


00:55:45.521 --> 00:55:47.201
there are none like that!


00:55:47.321 --> 00:55:51.641
<i>However, I felt sure
that the mass market</i>


00:55:51.761 --> 00:55:54.481
<i>would want to know about
how the universe began.</i>


00:55:55.641 --> 00:56:00.081
<i>By 1984,
I had completed the first chapter.</i>


00:56:00.201 --> 00:56:02.521
There was great interest in him.


00:56:02.641 --> 00:56:05.081
He was a great public figure,


00:56:05.201 --> 00:56:07.721
so actually every publisher in town
was interested in the book.


00:56:07.841 --> 00:56:10.161
I guess we had a contract
ready for him to sign


00:56:10.281 --> 00:56:14.361
and I had heard that he was
going to be in Chicago,


00:56:14.481 --> 00:56:16.961
so I was there and waiting,


00:56:17.081 --> 00:56:18.721
and then this car pulls
into the parking lot


00:56:18.841 --> 00:56:23.681
and this gentleman gets out
and he goes back to the passenger door


00:56:23.801 --> 00:56:28.401
and scoops out what looks like a kind of
life-size, broken doll into his arms,


00:56:28.521 --> 00:56:30.921
and brings it back to the wheelchair


00:56:31.041 --> 00:56:34.241
and kind of gently eases the doll
into place


00:56:34.361 --> 00:56:37.001
and suddenly
the doll becomes animated.


00:56:37.121 --> 00:56:39.681
As soon as that hand
is on the controls,


00:56:39.801 --> 00:56:44.841
the thing literally kicks into life, spins
around two, three times and takes off.


00:56:44.961 --> 00:56:46.881
And then Brian shouts to me,
I've gotten out of my car,


00:56:47.001 --> 00:56:50.001
he shouts, "Is that you?
Is that Peter Guzzardi?"


00:56:50.121 --> 00:56:51.721
and I said, "Yes, it's... it's me!"


00:56:51.841 --> 00:56:54.721
and he says, "Well, quick, follow us -
that's Professor Hawking!"


00:56:58.921 --> 00:57:00.521
<i>I signed up with Peter</i>


00:57:00.641 --> 00:57:04.121
<i>and set to work completing
the first draft of my book.</i>


00:57:05.401 --> 00:57:09.801
<i>I tried to simplify the physics
as best I could, and by the end</i>


00:57:09.921 --> 00:57:13.001
<i>I was pleased and felt
it was in pretty good shape.</i>


00:57:14.241 --> 00:57:16.961
<i>But Peter wasn't convinced.</i>


00:57:17.081 --> 00:57:20.481
I was pretty disappointed, yeah.


00:57:20.601 --> 00:57:22.961
I thought, "This is gonna be
really difficult."


00:57:24.081 --> 00:57:27.641
But I just decided we'd made
a substantial commitment to it


00:57:27.761 --> 00:57:29.441
and, by God,
we were gonna do this book,


00:57:29.561 --> 00:57:33.641
so let's just start slogging and then
maybe lightning would strike,


00:57:33.761 --> 00:57:35.841
or something wonderful would happen.


00:57:43.561 --> 00:57:45.801
<i>Lightning did indeed strike,</i>


00:57:45.921 --> 00:57:48.761
<i>but not in the way
that Peter and I were hoping.</i>


00:57:50.641 --> 00:57:54.201
<i>That summer,
I had taken a break from rewriting,</i>


00:57:54.321 --> 00:57:56.921
<i>to travel to Switzerland on holiday...</i>


00:57:58.281 --> 00:58:01.281
<i>...but while I was there
I caught a chest infection</i>


00:58:01.401 --> 00:58:06.161
<i>that developed into pneumonia
and quickly became very serious.</i>


00:58:10.041 --> 00:58:14.841
<i>I was put into a drug-induced coma
and onto a life-support machine.</i>


00:58:16.561 --> 00:58:19.161
<i>The doctors thought I was so far gone,</i>


00:58:19.281 --> 00:58:22.601
<i>they offered to Jane
to turn off the machine.</i>


00:58:23.841 --> 00:58:25.161
<i>But she refused.</i>


00:58:26.361 --> 00:58:32.041
<i>Finally, Jane insisted
that I was flown back to Cambridge.</i>


00:58:33.801 --> 00:58:35.761
I remember very vividly


00:58:35.881 --> 00:58:38.401
walking through the doors
and being really


00:58:38.521 --> 00:58:43.801
quite rocked by what I saw
when I got in there


00:58:43.921 --> 00:58:48.521
and I realised that Stephen was
really very, very ill.


00:58:49.561 --> 00:58:52.961
Things were not looking good.


00:58:55.401 --> 00:58:57.121
He could have died.


00:58:58.321 --> 00:59:01.081
We were just told
that he couldn't breathe


00:59:01.201 --> 00:59:04.241
and, of course, we knew
how weak he was at that time,


00:59:04.242 --> 00:59:06.442
and he had been ill before


00:59:06.561 --> 00:59:09.641
and we all knew
that he was on borrowed time.


00:59:13.281 --> 00:59:17.641
<i>The weeks of intensive care
were the darkest of my life.</i>


00:59:17.761 --> 00:59:22.921
<i>I felt I had always fought my illness
so hard that I was not prepared</i>


00:59:23.041 --> 00:59:26.081
<i>to give in so easily.</i>


00:59:27.441 --> 00:59:32.041
<i>Slowly, the drugs began to work
and the infection passed,</i>


00:59:32.161 --> 00:59:36.281
<i>but the surgeons had to perform a
tracheotomy to allow me to breathe,</i>


00:59:36.401 --> 00:59:40.401
<i>which made a small incision
in my windpipe</i>


00:59:40.521 --> 00:59:44.321
<i>and connected me to a ventilator
via the hole in my throat.</i>


00:59:46.161 --> 00:59:50.161
<i>As a result, I was now robbed
of the ability to talk.</i>


00:59:53.081 --> 00:59:56.961
<i>I faced a life
unable to properly communicate.</i>


00:59:57.081 --> 01:00:00.601
It was a very worrying time
for everyone around him


01:00:00.721 --> 01:00:03.801
and you're thinking, "Do you think
you'll ever be able to talk again?"


01:00:03.921 --> 01:00:06.521
or, "How will you work
if you can't talk?"


01:00:07.761 --> 01:00:09.641
So, yes, it was a very bleak time.


01:00:11.441 --> 01:00:15.841
<i>All hopes of finishing my book,
and perhaps even my career,</i>


01:00:15.961 --> 01:00:17.121
<i>seemed to be over.</i>


01:00:20.281 --> 01:00:23.561
It was very tense for everybody.
It was incredibly tense.


01:00:24.921 --> 01:00:29.201
It was difficult to think that Stephen
was going to come out of there


01:00:29.321 --> 01:00:31.001
and be OK.


01:00:40.241 --> 01:00:43.561
<i>As the weeks of my recovery
turned into months,</i>


01:00:43.681 --> 01:00:49.161
<i>it became obvious to me, and everyone
around me, that being on a ventilator</i>


01:00:49.281 --> 01:00:54.681
<i>meant that I needed constant care
and monitoring to keep alive.</i>


01:00:56.601 --> 01:01:01.041
This was a very
big realisation that


01:01:01.042 --> 01:01:04.042
things were going to dramatically change


01:01:04.161 --> 01:01:09.561
and that this was going to be needing
24-hour-a-day nursing care.


01:01:10.721 --> 01:01:15.201
Their lives were never, ever
going to be the same again.


01:01:17.521 --> 01:01:22.601
Once nurses came into the house...
life changed.


01:01:24.201 --> 01:01:28.761
And that was very difficult
for all of us - for me, for the children.


01:01:29.961 --> 01:01:31.801
Home was no longer home.


01:01:33.961 --> 01:01:36.681
There was no privacy, no privacy,


01:01:36.801 --> 01:01:40.601
because the walls
were listening to everything.


01:01:44.481 --> 01:01:47.161
<i>Despite the intrusions
on family life,</i>


01:01:47.281 --> 01:01:50.681
<i>with the nurses' help,
I grew stronger.</i>


01:01:56.601 --> 01:02:00.241
<i>Yet I still felt trapped
inside my body.</i>


01:02:00.361 --> 01:02:04.361
<i>For a time, at home,
I could communicate</i>


01:02:04.481 --> 01:02:06.601
<i>only by raising my eyebrows</i>


01:02:06.721 --> 01:02:11.041
<i>when someone pointed to letters
on a card.</i>


01:02:11.161 --> 01:02:15.681
<i>All thoughts of work
and finishing my book grew distant.</i>


01:02:22.361 --> 01:02:26.481
<i>But then, quite unexpectedly,
a glimmer of hope</i>


01:02:26.601 --> 01:02:29.441
<i>came from across the water.</i>


01:02:31.041 --> 01:02:35.121
I got a call from a physicist
and he said, "I know


01:02:35.241 --> 01:02:38.961
"you're working on computer systems
for people with ALS."


01:02:39.081 --> 01:02:41.441
He says, "I've got someone in England,


01:02:41.561 --> 01:02:43.361
"he's a professor of physics,


01:02:43.481 --> 01:02:47.601
"who lost the ability to speak
and he needs a system."


01:02:49.321 --> 01:02:50.761
The system was called Equalizer


01:02:50.881 --> 01:02:54.281
and the top part of the screen
was a set of letters,


01:02:54.401 --> 01:02:58.881
rows of letters, and then the bottom part
of the screen was rows of words -


01:02:59.001 --> 01:03:01.441
36 very frequently used words,


01:03:01.561 --> 01:03:06.041
so he could choose
the top part or the bottom part.


01:03:06.161 --> 01:03:09.601
He learned that very quickly
and I was blown away by it.


01:03:09.721 --> 01:03:13.441
He was... He was scanning
just amazingly fast.


01:03:15.881 --> 01:03:19.041
<i>I had enough movement
in my right hand</i>


01:03:19.161 --> 01:03:23.481
<i>to be able to click the computer system
and write the words I wanted.</i>


01:03:25.481 --> 01:03:28.681
<i>Finally, I was free
to communicate again.</i>


01:03:30.121 --> 01:03:36.281
<i>I was keen to make up the lost time
that my illness had forced upon me.</i>


01:03:36.401 --> 01:03:40.081
<i>I had a stack of notes
from Peter Guzzardi</i>


01:03:40.201 --> 01:03:44.601
<i>suggesting changes
and clarifications to my book,</i>


01:03:44.721 --> 01:03:48.801
<i>but I needed practical help
with the rewrite at my end,</i>


01:03:48.921 --> 01:03:51.561
<i>someone who could act
as a go-between.</i>


01:03:51.681 --> 01:03:54.601
<i>...is keeping the graphic
as simple as we can.</i>


01:03:54.721 --> 01:03:56.081
The people in the States


01:03:56.201 --> 01:03:58.521
were speaking on a loudspeaker phone
in his office


01:03:58.641 --> 01:04:03.801
and Stephen was writing, using
his new computer system on the screen


01:04:03.921 --> 01:04:08.161
and then you would say
what he was saying over the phone.


01:04:08.281 --> 01:04:10.921
We were just sort of
cobbling it together,


01:04:11.041 --> 01:04:13.121
I think, is probably the right term.


01:04:13.241 --> 01:04:18.081
I had no idea what was going on
on Stephen's side of this,


01:04:18.201 --> 01:04:22.001
where it seems that he was persuading
Stephen that this was OK,


01:04:22.121 --> 01:04:26.321
helping Stephen off the walls
if Stephen started to think,


01:04:26.441 --> 01:04:28.841
"Why the hell
am I spending all this time


01:04:28.961 --> 01:04:31.881
"making this idiot
understand this basic stuff?"


01:04:32.161 --> 01:04:36.361
<i>After months of work,
the rewrite was complete.</i>


01:04:36.481 --> 01:04:40.161
<i>None of us really knew
whether the book would be liked</i>


01:04:40.281 --> 01:04:42.641
<i>and would sell as we all hoped for.</i>


01:04:42.761 --> 01:04:46.161
<i>All we could do now
was give it a title -</i>


01:04:46.281 --> 01:04:51.721
A Brief History Of Time -
<i>send it off to the printers and wait.</i>


01:04:56.761 --> 01:05:02.481
<i>But to everyone's surprise
the book sold copy after copy</i>


01:05:02.601 --> 01:05:05.881
<i>and very quickly
bookshops were selling out.</i>


01:05:06.001 --> 01:05:08.441
When it hit the bestseller list,
you're obviously surprised.


01:05:08.561 --> 01:05:10.961
It was a pleasant surprise
and it certainly was a surprise.


01:05:11.081 --> 01:05:13.801
I don't think you'll find anybody -
maybe I'm wrong - who'll say,


01:05:13.921 --> 01:05:16.481
"'Oh, yes, we knew all along
this was going to be a major hit."


01:05:16.601 --> 01:05:18.641
I had no expectation
that it was going to be


01:05:18.761 --> 01:05:21.401
the number-one bestselling book
in the world.


01:05:21.521 --> 01:05:24.161
I mean, not just here,
but in Germany, Slovenia,


01:05:24.281 --> 01:05:28.401
France, Italy - everywhere in the world
there was the hope


01:05:28.521 --> 01:05:31.521
that someone had found
the mystery of life.


01:05:31.641 --> 01:05:35.481
And from then on it was just a race
to keep the book in print


01:05:35.601 --> 01:05:39.441
and marching towards
a million copies sold.


01:05:39.561 --> 01:05:42.201
You know, it was very gratifying!


01:05:42.321 --> 01:05:46.681
Er, you know, in the 38 years
that I've been in this business,


01:05:46.801 --> 01:05:51.121
um...I don't think
I've ever had a book


01:05:51.241 --> 01:05:54.921
that stayed at the top
of the bestseller list that long.


01:05:55.041 --> 01:05:59.681
Well, I was amazed
at how well it did. I think it worked.


01:05:59.801 --> 01:06:01.081
He inspired people.


01:06:01.201 --> 01:06:05.081
He gave people some overall sense
of the birth of the universe


01:06:05.201 --> 01:06:10.801
and it made this subject become
a subject of conversation


01:06:10.921 --> 01:06:13.201
among people in all walks of life.


01:06:13.321 --> 01:06:15.961
Professor Stephen Hawking's book


01:06:16.081 --> 01:06:17.961
<i>A Brief History Of Time,</i>
an unlikely


01:06:18.081 --> 01:06:19.801
but successful
publishing phenomenon...


01:06:19.921 --> 01:06:23.641
<i>A Brief History Of Time</i>
has sold about eight million copies...


01:06:23.761 --> 01:06:25.281
A popular book
about his theories


01:06:25.401 --> 01:06:28.321
is already topping
the American bestsellers' list...


01:06:28.441 --> 01:06:30.281
The hugely successful
<i>A Brief History Of Time</i>


01:06:30.401 --> 01:06:31.841
by Stephen Hawking...


01:06:33.281 --> 01:06:35.961
A Brief History Of Time
<i>stayed in the bestseller list</i>


01:06:36.081 --> 01:06:38.081
<i>for over four years</i>


01:06:38.201 --> 01:06:41.961
<i>and entered into</i> The Guinness Book
Of Records <i>for doing so.</i>


01:06:42.081 --> 01:06:45.881
<i>To date, over 10 million copies
have been sold worldwide.</i>


01:06:54.201 --> 01:06:59.121
<i>Over the next few years,
a lot of fuss was made about my book.</i>


01:06:59.241 --> 01:07:02.801
<i>I became famous nationally
and around the world,</i>


01:07:02.921 --> 01:07:06.241
<i>as it was translated
into 40 different languages.</i>


01:07:06.361 --> 01:07:09.321
...delighted to welcome
Professor Stephen Hawking.


01:07:12.241 --> 01:07:14.641
<i>I was invited onto chat shows...</i>


01:07:14.761 --> 01:07:17.481
Well, I think people do think of you
as a genius,


01:07:17.601 --> 01:07:19.841
not just as a disabled genius.


01:07:19.961 --> 01:07:23.721
<i>...and I've made
a cameo appearance on Star Trek,</i>


01:07:23.841 --> 01:07:26.161
<i>my favourite sci-fi show.</i>


01:07:26.281 --> 01:07:30.401
- You are bluffing.
- Wrong again, Albert!


01:07:34.281 --> 01:07:37.641
<i>I enjoyed the media attention</i>


01:07:37.761 --> 01:07:42.441
<i>and witnessing everyday people
getting more involved in understanding</i>


01:07:42.561 --> 01:07:45.281
<i>the physics of our universe.</i>


01:07:47.481 --> 01:07:52.921
<i>But soon the press wanted to know
more about me, my illness</i>


01:07:53.041 --> 01:07:55.041
<i>and my family life.</i>


01:08:01.601 --> 01:08:05.521
He wanted this
worldwide celebrity, he enjoys that.


01:08:05.641 --> 01:08:09.481
Part of his whole outlook on life
and his science is that it's fun


01:08:09.601 --> 01:08:10.881
and celebrity was fun


01:08:11.001 --> 01:08:12.481
and he embraced it


01:08:12.601 --> 01:08:16.681
in a way that was not necessarily
very good for the family, the children.


01:08:18.241 --> 01:08:22.321
So this was a further real problem
in the marriage.


01:08:24.681 --> 01:08:28.041
We were engulfed and swept away


01:08:28.161 --> 01:08:33.321
by this great wave
of fame and fortune...


01:08:37.361 --> 01:08:38.601
...and I have to say


01:08:38.721 --> 01:08:42.841
that really it all got rather too much
for me to cope with


01:08:42.961 --> 01:08:49.241
and I suppose that's when we ceased
to be as happy as we had been


01:08:49.361 --> 01:08:52.361
and when the marriage broke up


01:08:52.481 --> 01:08:55.721
I felt as if a rug,


01:08:55.841 --> 01:08:59.201
not just had been pulled up
from under my feet,


01:08:59.321 --> 01:09:03.001
but the earth had opened up
under that rug and swallowed me up,


01:09:03.121 --> 01:09:06.161
because the marriage had
been my raison d'étre.


01:09:07.761 --> 01:09:13.321
And it took me quite a little while
to recover my sense of my own identity.


01:09:21.881 --> 01:09:24.921
<i>It was clear
that my life and Jane's</i>


01:09:25.041 --> 01:09:27.841
<i>were beginning to follow
different paths.</i>


01:09:28.881 --> 01:09:34.881
<i>In 1990 we separated,
and were divorced in 1995.</i>


01:09:40.961 --> 01:09:45.001
<i>In the same year, I announced
my engagement to Elaine Mason</i>


01:09:45.121 --> 01:09:46.521
<i>and married again.</i>


01:09:48.641 --> 01:09:51.441
<i>Elaine had been one of my nurses</i>


01:09:51.561 --> 01:09:54.721
<i>from the start
of my 24-hour wraparound care.</i>


01:09:56.681 --> 01:10:01.201
<i>Over the years, we
had become extremely close</i>


01:10:01.202 --> 01:10:03.402
<i>as Jane and I drifted apart,</i>


01:10:03.521 --> 01:10:06.881
<i>each seeking comfort and love
through new relationships.</i>


01:10:12.081 --> 01:10:16.881
Elaine became closer to Stephen
than any of the other nurses.


01:10:20.961 --> 01:10:25.361
When he travelled, he would prefer
to have her with him over the others.


01:10:25.481 --> 01:10:27.561
It became a close relationship.


01:10:30.801 --> 01:10:36.041
<i>My marriage to Elaine
was passionate and tempestuous.</i>


01:10:36.161 --> 01:10:39.761
<i>She saved my life
on several occasions</i>


01:10:39.881 --> 01:10:46.601
<i>and we were together for 11 years,
before divorcing in 2006.</i>


01:10:47.921 --> 01:10:52.361
<i>The low point was when the press
printed unsubstantiated allegations</i>


01:10:52.481 --> 01:10:55.321
<i>that I had been the victim
of domestic violence.</i>


01:10:56.921 --> 01:11:01.321
<i>To my mind, this was
a gross invasion of our privacy</i>


01:11:01.441 --> 01:11:05.001
<i>and it was an extremely hurtful
and damaging time for us both.</i>


01:11:06.041 --> 01:11:10.481
<i>Unfortunately, being in the public eye
can have its drawbacks.</i>


01:11:31.881 --> 01:11:36.361
<i>Living a very public life does, however,
have its upsides too.</i>


01:11:37.721 --> 01:11:42.161
<i>At work, I am often visited
by famous people who share</i>


01:11:42.281 --> 01:11:45.881
<i>an interest in space
and the universe we live in.</i>


01:11:46.001 --> 01:11:49.401
<i>Sometimes, even astronauts drop by.</i>


01:11:52.201 --> 01:11:58.401
My voice is listened to because
I did something 43 years ago


01:11:58.521 --> 01:12:02.161
and I have the sense of humans


01:12:02.281 --> 01:12:04.841
reaching outward and succeeding,


01:12:04.961 --> 01:12:09.401
but I believe
that he is valued more because


01:12:09.521 --> 01:12:14.561
he has the pure analytical
combined with


01:12:14.681 --> 01:12:16.201
the philosophical


01:12:16.321 --> 01:12:18.841
that comes from his understanding


01:12:18.961 --> 01:12:22.721
of the beginnings and the ends
of the universe.


01:12:28.161 --> 01:12:32.401
<i>I have often dreamt about
travelling through space myself.</i>


01:12:35.641 --> 01:12:41.361
<i>Recently, I got closer by
experiencing a zero gravity flight.</i>


01:12:43.561 --> 01:12:49.161
<i>It was also a moment that temporarily
stripped me of my disability</i> -


01:12:49.281 --> 01:12:52.201
<i>a feeling of true freedom.</i>


01:13:37.201 --> 01:13:41.321
<i>I believe the pioneering efforts
of the early astronauts like Buzz</i>


01:13:41.441 --> 01:13:44.281
<i>need to be taken onto the next level.</i>


01:13:46.041 --> 01:13:47.721
<i>I am convinced that one day</i>


01:13:47.841 --> 01:13:52.401
<i>humans will have to colonise
other planets in order to survive.</i>


01:13:56.321 --> 01:13:59.681
<i>Space travel will become
an everyday necessity.</i>


01:14:03.921 --> 01:14:07.881
<i>Until then, I hope to be
one of the first ordinary people</i>


01:14:08.001 --> 01:14:09.561
<i>to blast off into space.</i>


01:14:09.681 --> 01:14:12.561
<i>But I will need a little help.</i>


01:14:18.561 --> 01:14:22.161
I just couldn't think
of anybody in the world


01:14:22.281 --> 01:14:25.321
that we'd rather send to space
than Stephen Hawking,


01:14:25.441 --> 01:14:27.921
and, you know, we haven't offered
anybody a free ticket,


01:14:28.041 --> 01:14:30.441
but it was the one person in the world
that we felt, you know,


01:14:30.561 --> 01:14:32.241
we'd love to invite you to space.


01:14:36.481 --> 01:14:37.481
Pressure check.


01:14:39.041 --> 01:14:42.281
And it was incredible when he accepted
and I went up and saw him that day


01:14:42.401 --> 01:14:43.881
and he told me to hurry up


01:14:44.001 --> 01:14:47.481
and get the spaceship built
because he wasn't going to live forever,


01:14:47.601 --> 01:14:50.641
and hopefully next year we'll take
him up and I think that, you know,


01:14:50.761 --> 01:14:54.481
he feels that if he goes into space
personally, he can...he can lead the way.


01:14:58.401 --> 01:15:02.721
<i>My hopes for space travel
have been with me throughout my life.</i>


01:15:04.001 --> 01:15:08.041
<i>But as my wheels are still firmly
planted here on Planet Earth,</i>


01:15:08.161 --> 01:15:09.601
<i>I will keep on dreaming.</i>


01:15:17.281 --> 01:15:21.361
<i>As I am now considered
a so-called "famous" person,</i>


01:15:21.481 --> 01:15:24.921
<i>more and more of my time is taken up
with my public life.</i>


01:15:26.481 --> 01:15:31.161
<i>For this, I rely greatly
upon my team around me.</i>


01:15:33.241 --> 01:15:37.441
You are really
the gatekeeper in this role.


01:15:37.561 --> 01:15:41.001
You get the general fan mail,
hero-worshipping him, really,


01:15:41.121 --> 01:15:42.641
then you get people who are obsessed


01:15:42.761 --> 01:15:47.601
with religion and God and the fact that
Professor Hawking doesn't appear to have


01:15:47.721 --> 01:15:52.081
a god as such that they can,
um, feel comfortable about,


01:15:52.201 --> 01:15:55.601
so you get a huge range
of what we call the "god letters",


01:15:55.721 --> 01:15:59.281
and then you get letters
from the disabled,


01:15:59.401 --> 01:16:02.841
serious scientists - mostly people


01:16:02.961 --> 01:16:07.401
full of admiration for Professor Hawking
and what he has achieved.


01:16:07.521 --> 01:16:09.241
He is a great iconic figure


01:16:09.361 --> 01:16:13.281
and he enjoys his public appearances


01:16:13.401 --> 01:16:15.921
and it's great
that, you know, he's still very engaged


01:16:16.041 --> 01:16:18.721
with everything that surrounds him.


01:16:22.721 --> 01:16:27.041
So if you want to explore
the inside of a black hole,


01:16:27.161 --> 01:16:28.721
choose a big one.


01:16:31.161 --> 01:16:34.361
People have searched
for many black holes of this mass


01:16:34.481 --> 01:16:37.041
but have, so far, not found any.


01:16:37.161 --> 01:16:44.081
This is a pity because, if they had,
I would have got a Nobel Prize.


01:16:49.681 --> 01:16:52.401
<i>If you had asked me 40 years ago</i>


01:16:52.521 --> 01:16:56.361
<i>if I ever thought I would be talking
in front of a sell-out crowd</i>


01:16:56.481 --> 01:17:00.521
<i>and be a part of popular culture,
I would have laughed.</i>


01:17:01.641 --> 01:17:06.881
<i>But if my getting involved
with the zeitgeist that is upon us</i>


01:17:07.001 --> 01:17:11.961
<i>has encouraged people to question
our universe, then I am OK.</i>


01:17:13.001 --> 01:17:14.641
Stephen Hawking!


01:17:14.761 --> 01:17:17.441
- The world's smartest man.
- What are you doing here?


01:17:17.561 --> 01:17:20.681
If you are looking for trouble,
you've found it.


01:17:20.801 --> 01:17:22.601
Yeah, just try me, you... Oh!


01:17:25.561 --> 01:17:29.241
He's willing to teach people
in whatever way he has to teach people -


01:17:29.361 --> 01:17:33.041
you can't take yourself too seriously
no matter how intelligent


01:17:33.161 --> 01:17:35.521
or how important the work you're doing is.


01:17:35.641 --> 01:17:37.321
- This is Jim's phone.
- It's for me!


01:17:37.441 --> 01:17:39.921
- It's such an honour. It's for you.
- Hello!


01:17:40.041 --> 01:17:43.201
Whenever I do a talk show,
I'm trying to think of ways to do things


01:17:43.321 --> 01:17:47.161
that are completely different
and completely ridiculous.


01:17:47.281 --> 01:17:49.681
Most people see <i>Dumb & Dumber</i>
and they assume that that's who I am,


01:17:49.801 --> 01:17:53.561
so I thought, "Well, wouldn't it be
wonderful to, like, partner up


01:17:53.681 --> 01:17:55.641
"with the smartest man on the Earth?"


01:17:55.761 --> 01:17:57.681
Oh, excuse me!


01:17:57.801 --> 01:17:59.441
Hello.


01:18:04.201 --> 01:18:07.001
Oh, hi, Stephen Hawking!
I can't believe it!


01:18:07.121 --> 01:18:09.641
He was wonderful.
He was just fantastic.


01:18:09.761 --> 01:18:13.481
I was... I was expecting him
to be so serious about himself


01:18:13.601 --> 01:18:16.081
and, er, I think it was a relief for him


01:18:16.201 --> 01:18:19.441
to, er... to completely make fun
of the whole thing.


01:18:27.281 --> 01:18:28.721
It's amazing.


01:18:36.521 --> 01:18:37.441
Yeah, I know.


01:18:37.561 --> 01:18:41.001
He did a great job of doing the lines
that I'd written


01:18:41.121 --> 01:18:44.801
and... and, er, and he was just
gung ho the whole time.


01:18:46.801 --> 01:18:47.801
OK.


01:18:54.961 --> 01:18:55.961
Oh!


01:18:57.521 --> 01:18:59.041
No, <i>you're</i> a genius!


01:19:00.281 --> 01:19:02.001
No! You are!


01:19:02.121 --> 01:19:05.481
After we did the routine,
we kind of struck up a friendship,


01:19:05.601 --> 01:19:08.161
so I was invited to his home


01:19:08.281 --> 01:19:10.321
and one of the funny highlights
of the night


01:19:10.441 --> 01:19:12.881
was that, er... was that
while we were having dinner


01:19:13.001 --> 01:19:14.801
I asked him, you know,
just for a picture,


01:19:14.921 --> 01:19:17.241
"Could you run over my foot
with your wheelchair?"


01:19:17.361 --> 01:19:21.361
And that... So I have a picture of me
grimacing in agony and stuff,


01:19:21.481 --> 01:19:25.761
so it was wonderful, wonderful to be
there, wonderful to talk to him


01:19:25.881 --> 01:19:27.961
and ask him
important questions like,


01:19:28.081 --> 01:19:31.921
"How many Higgs boson
does it take to screw in a light bulb?'


01:19:32.041 --> 01:19:33.801
And, er, I think
he's still struggling with that.


01:19:36.881 --> 01:19:38.921
Look out!


01:19:42.521 --> 01:19:46.281
<i>It has been a lot of fun
and also very strange</i>


01:19:46.401 --> 01:19:49.921
<i>to see myself depicted in so many ways,</i>


01:19:50.041 --> 01:19:53.361
<i>but perhaps the strangest
is to have had</i>


01:19:53.481 --> 01:19:56.401
<i>part of my early life
portrayed by an actor.</i>


01:19:57.721 --> 01:20:01.721
I felt a huge onus of responsibility
to get that part of his life right.


01:20:01.841 --> 01:20:03.361
There was so much that
happened to him.


01:20:03.481 --> 01:20:05.401
It's a terrifying prospect


01:20:05.521 --> 01:20:08.521
to have a completely functioning mind
inside a body


01:20:08.641 --> 01:20:12.441
that locks you in,
that keeps you stationary.


01:20:25.121 --> 01:20:28.281
One of the things I wanted to get right
was to show the stages


01:20:28.401 --> 01:20:34.081
of the progression of his condition and
where the instability and fear came from.


01:20:34.201 --> 01:20:37.481
The very obvious details, such as being
at the top of a flight of stairs,


01:20:37.601 --> 01:20:40.321
that suddenly becomes the most
enormous obstacle,


01:20:40.441 --> 01:20:44.961
being on any sort of uneven ground -
those feelings of vulnerability.


01:20:46.281 --> 01:20:47.721
He's incredibly stoic.


01:20:47.841 --> 01:20:50.121
I think that was probably the case
when he was younger as well.


01:20:50.241 --> 01:20:52.441
I think he rolled up his sleeves
and got on with it,


01:20:52.561 --> 01:20:54.081
and look at the results.


01:20:54.201 --> 01:20:56.041
I mean, it's self-evident -


01:20:56.161 --> 01:21:00.881
the man became, er, a spokesperson
for the most complex ideas.


01:21:18.001 --> 01:21:19.521
Right, Stephen, we're going to put


01:21:19.641 --> 01:21:21.521
your hat on you, so you don't get wet.


01:21:22.721 --> 01:21:25.881
The straps go round,
so it holds the hat on.


01:21:27.281 --> 01:21:31.561
- Look at you!
- He thinks we're mean.


01:21:31.681 --> 01:21:33.401
"Have you got a hat?"
"Yeah, gangster hat."


01:21:33.521 --> 01:21:36.841
It's the don!


01:21:36.961 --> 01:21:40.281
<i>One advantage of being
a public figure</i>


01:21:40.401 --> 01:21:42.881
<i>is being asked to do special things.</i>


01:21:43.001 --> 01:21:49.001
<i>Today I am centre stage at the opening
ceremony of the Paralympics Games</i>


01:21:49.121 --> 01:21:52.041
<i>and it's an honour to do so.


01:22:03.721 --> 01:22:06.241
Ever since the dawn of civilisation,


01:22:06.361 --> 01:22:12.241
people have craved for an understanding
of the underlying order of the world.


01:22:13.561 --> 01:22:17.281
There ought to be something very
special about the boundary conditions


01:22:17.401 --> 01:22:19.241
of the universe...


01:22:21.081 --> 01:22:24.401
...and what can be more special than that
there is no boundary?


01:22:27.921 --> 01:22:31.521
And there should be no boundary
to human endeavour.


01:22:34.441 --> 01:22:39.161
The Paralympic Games are all about
transforming our perception of the world.


01:22:40.481 --> 01:22:41.881
We are all different.


01:22:42.001 --> 01:22:47.081
There is no such thing as a standard
or run-of-the-mill human being,


01:22:47.201 --> 01:22:49.321
but we share the same human spirit.


01:22:57.801 --> 01:23:00.561
However difficult life may seem,


01:23:00.681 --> 01:23:04.841
there is always something you can do
and succeed at.


01:23:06.881 --> 01:23:10.801
The Games provide an opportunity
for athletes to excel,


01:23:10.921 --> 01:23:15.041
to stretch themselves
and become outstanding in their field.


01:23:17.081 --> 01:23:21.841
So let us together celebrate
excellence, friendship and respect.


01:23:24.761 --> 01:23:26.641
Good luck to you all.


01:23:45.401 --> 01:23:48.041
Oh! Goodness!


01:23:57.561 --> 01:23:59.481
Oh!


01:24:05.041 --> 01:24:10.041
<i>Having lived on this
wonderful planet for over 71 years,</i>


01:24:10.161 --> 01:24:12.841
<i>I feel my proudest achievement
has been to inspire people</i>


01:24:12.961 --> 01:24:16.081
<i>to think about the cosmos
and our place in it.</i>


01:24:17.521 --> 01:24:19.681
<i>Since I believe
there is no afterlife,</i>


01:24:19.801 --> 01:24:22.721
<i>I think it's important to realise</i>


01:24:22.841 --> 01:24:27.721
<i>we only have a very short time alive
and should make the best of it.</i>


01:24:29.001 --> 01:24:32.961
<i>Today, I enjoy time together
with family and friends...</i>


01:24:34.001 --> 01:24:36.201
<i>...and despite my disabilities</i>


01:24:36.321 --> 01:24:41.561
<i>I'll always keep wondering
about the mysteries of the universe.</i>


01:24:41.681 --> 01:24:46.201
Stephen's been ill since he was 20.


01:24:46.321 --> 01:24:50.761
You come to terms with
the impending bereavement


01:24:50.881 --> 01:24:55.841
and then suddenly find
that it's not that impending after all.


01:25:04.001 --> 01:25:06.881
I'm sure there's plenty more
he'd like to know about.


01:25:07.001 --> 01:25:10.241
Think how awful it would be
to come to the end of questions.


01:25:12.641 --> 01:25:17.721
Stephen will carry on until they...
push him underground.


01:25:19.641 --> 01:25:22.121
You... You don't just stop your mind.


01:25:25.441 --> 01:25:30.281
Stephen's always had
this determination to survive, I guess,


01:25:30.401 --> 01:25:33.641
and every time Stephen gets
critically ill, I always think


01:25:33.761 --> 01:25:36.361
"Oh, dear, I hope," you know,
"he's going to get through this time,"


01:25:36.481 --> 01:25:38.441
and he always does.


01:25:38.561 --> 01:25:41.041
He's just got this tremendous
determination to live


01:25:41.161 --> 01:25:44.441
and I... and I do think there is
such a thing as the will to live.


01:25:45.881 --> 01:25:48.721
There are several components
to his long survival.


01:25:48.841 --> 01:25:52.641
One is
that he's, by a very large margin,


01:25:52.761 --> 01:25:55.721
the most stubborn person
I've ever met.


01:25:57.841 --> 01:26:00.481
He is one of those very few people


01:26:00.601 --> 01:26:05.281
who can see far beyond
the borders of current knowledge


01:26:05.401 --> 01:26:09.201
and see how things really work
and set the directions


01:26:09.321 --> 01:26:13.681
for other people's research and
attempts to prove him right or wrong.


01:26:17.601 --> 01:26:21.561
To recover from a broken marriage,
it was very, very difficult.


01:26:23.041 --> 01:26:26.601
At first, we were in touch...


01:26:27.681 --> 01:26:29.681
...but then it got very, very difficult,


01:26:29.801 --> 01:26:33.841
but, lately, we have been able
to associate.


01:26:33.961 --> 01:26:37.321
I call on him
perhaps once a fortnight.


01:26:38.761 --> 01:26:42.441
All those years with Stephen
were a huge part of my life.


01:26:42.561 --> 01:26:44.961
They were my young years.


01:26:45.081 --> 01:26:47.401
They were my children.


01:26:47.521 --> 01:26:52.721
And I can't just wipe those
from the record, and I wouldn't want to.


01:26:52.841 --> 01:26:57.281
It would be self-destructive
to wipe those away


01:26:57.401 --> 01:27:00.721
and I'm very, very proud of what
Stephen has done.


01:27:04.761 --> 01:27:09.561
<i>Life has thrown at me
both good times and bad.</i>


01:27:09.681 --> 01:27:14.921
<i>Perhaps it is human nature
that we adapt and survive.</i>


01:27:18.201 --> 01:27:20.281
<i>As for me,</i>


01:27:20.401 --> 01:27:24.121
<i>I have lived with the prospect
of an early death most of my life.</i>


01:27:25.201 --> 01:27:29.841
<i>I am not afraid of dying,
but I'm in no hurry to die.</i>


01:27:31.081 --> 01:27:34.441
<i>I have so much I want to do
and find out first.</i>


01:27:39.641 --> 01:27:41.041
<i>So, for now...</i>


01:27:42.441 --> 01:27:47.361
<i>...goodbye and thank you for coming
on a journey through my world.</i>


01:27:57.561 --> 01:28:01.161
<i># Zauberfest</i>


01:28:01.281 --> 01:28:04.601
<i># Bezähmt ein Schlaf</i>


01:28:04.721 --> 01:28:11.001
<i># Der Holden Schmerz und Harm</i>


01:28:17.401 --> 01:28:23.361
<i># Da die Walküre zu mir frat</i>


01:28:23.481 --> 01:28:32.281
<i># Schuf sie ihr den wonnigen Trost?</i>


01:28:35.201 --> 01:28:40.201
<i># Sollte die grimmige Wal</i>


01:28:40.321 --> 01:28:48.881
<i># Nicht schrecken ein gramvolles Weib?</i>


01:28:58.561 --> 01:29:08.801
<i># Leblos scheint sie, die dennoch lebt</i>


01:29:11.121 --> 01:29:24.401
<i># Der Traurigen kost ein lächelnder Traum</i>


01:29:39.721 --> 01:29:48.201
<i># So schlummre nun fort
Bis die Schlacht gekämpft</i>


01:29:48.321 --> 01:29:57.921
<i># Und Friede dich erfreu'! #</i>


